## Problem

When a navigation keeps the current content on screen while its loader re-runs (a `transition()` same-route nav such as `/products/1 -> /products/2`, a same-structure search/filter nav), the `useLoader` reader that is still showing the OLD data had no signal that a replacement was in flight: `isLoading` only ever reflected client-initiated `load()`/`refetch()`. A consumer could not dim, badge, or otherwise mark the held data as stale; the only page-level signal was `useNavigation().state`, which cannot say WHICH loader is being replaced.

## Before / after

```tsx
"use client";
function ProductPrice() {
  const { data, isLoading } = useLoader(ProductLoader);
  // BEFORE: on /products/1 -> /products/2 (transition() route) the held
  // reader rendered `isLoading: false` the whole time and then swapped data.
  // AFTER:  it renders `isLoading: true` from the moment the new tree is
  // committed (inside the transition) until the commit that swaps `data`.
  return <span style={{ opacity: isLoading ? 0.5 : 1 }}>${data.price}</span>;
}
```

## End-to-end example

```tsx
// urls.tsx
path("/products/:id", ProductPage, { name: "product" }, () => [
  loader(ProductLoader),
  loading(<ProductSkeleton />),
  transition(), // hold the current product while the next one loads
]);

// ProductPage.tsx (server)
export async function ProductPage(ctx) {
  const product = await ctx.use(ProductLoader);
  return (
    <article>
      <h1>{product.name}</h1>
      <ProductPrice />
      <Link to={href("product", { id: String(product.id + 1) })}>Next</Link>
    </article>
  );
}

// ProductPrice.tsx (client)
"use client";
export function ProductPrice() {
  const { data, isLoading } = useLoader(ProductLoader);
  return (
    <p aria-busy={isLoading} className={isLoading ? "stale" : undefined}>
      {data.price} {isLoading && <span>updating…</span>}
    </p>
  );
}
```

Observed sequence on the held reader: `fresh:Product 1 -> stale:Product 1 -> fresh:Product 2`. Never `fresh:Product 1` again in between (the flag flips in the same commit that swaps the data).

## Semantics

| Situation | `isLoading` on the reader still on screen |
| --- | --- |
| Held nav (transition() same-route, same-structure search nav, fully-prefetched, optimistic clientUrls presentation) while this loader's stream is pending | `true` until the commit that brings the new data (unchanged: `load()`/`refetch()` states) |
| Loader the navigation does NOT re-run (unchanged layout loader; cached/reused segment) | `false`, data untouched (pinned by test: `loadedAt` byte-identical) |
| Stream settled before the commit (fully-prefetched, `{ ssr: false }` awaited, forceAwait lanes) | `false` (settled Flight chunk skipped) |
| Cold nav that mounts new segments (urgent commit) | unchanged: the read suspends to its `loading()`/Suspense fallback; no flag |
| Ephemeral `useFetchLoader` read outside route context | unchanged: never pinned (subscribes without `onStreamPending`) |
| Action / stale-revalidation lanes | unchanged: forceAwait commits settled data, announce is a no-op |
| Overlapping navs (second click before the first stream lands) | `true` until the LAST pending stream settles (Set per loader family) |

## Code change

- `src/browser/partial-update.ts` — every transition commit now goes through one local `commitInTransition(segments, update, transitionTypes)` (7 hand-copied `startTransition` blocks collapsed), which calls `loaderStore.announcePendingStreams(segments)` INSIDE the `startTransition`. Urgent commits call `onUpdate` directly.
- `src/loader-store.ts` — `pendingStreams: Map<loaderId, Set<Promise>>` (settle-released), `trackPendingStream`, `isStreamPending`, `announcePendingStreams(segments)` (registers loader segments whose `loaderData` is still a pending promise, then fires each subscriber's `onStreamPending` through the existing family index), new `SubscribeOptions.onStreamPending`.
- `src/use-loader.tsx` — `useOptimistic(false)` pin; the existing store subscription passes `onStreamPending: () => startTransition(() => setStreamPending(true))` for route-context readers; `isLoading = local || shared || streamPending`.
- `src/thenable-status.ts` — `unwrapsSynchronously` lifted out of `ssr-suspension-warning.ts` so the diagnostic and the store agree on "settled Flight chunk".

Why `useOptimistic` (both alternatives were built and measured through the e2e log): an urgent flag released when the stream settles logged `fresh:1, stale:1, fresh:1, fresh:2` — the stream settles a beat before React commits the held tree; a transition-lane release rendered alone because React excludes the suspended navigation lane from the transition batch. An optimistic update made inside the commit's transition is reverted by React exactly at that commit, which is the only thing that lines up with the data swap (same mechanism `useNavigation` uses).

Why the commit site, not the tree build: `renderSegments` runs on the server too, and "register here, announce there" split one mechanism across two files; the reconciled loader segments carry the same promises, so register + announce is one browser-only call.

