## Summary

Refines the target mutation policy so ordinary shutdown cannot accidentally interrupt programming, while preserving an explicit safety escape hatch.

### What it changes
- normal `power off` now participates in the same per-target mutation gate as power-on, flash and reset;
- normal shutdown during an active mutation is rejected immediately with API code `busy`;
- new explicit `EmergencyPowerOff` Runtime operation bypasses the mutation gate;
- new `/api/v1/power/emergency-off` endpoint;
- new `Benchpilot.Client.EmergencyPowerOff` operation;
- new CLI command `benchpilot power emergency-off`;
- new MCP `EmergencyPowerOff` tool whose description makes the interruption risk explicit;
- tests distinguish ordinary shutdown from emergency shutdown during an active flash.

## Safety rationale

The previous implementation let every normal `power off` bypass target locking. That made emergency shutdown possible, but also allowed an ordinary shell command to cut power in the middle of a flash. This PR narrows the bypass to a deliberately named emergency operation only.

## Product boundary

No CAN/UDS changes. This is runtime safety hardening before physical ECU validation.

